<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  RM_BlueIceMineable.xml                     BLUEDESERT_RULED_CONTENT_1 §3

  RM_BlueIce — the sheet's ⭐ "golden" mineable (the_blue_desert.md §7: "Blue
  ice — mineable water-ice blocks of distilled-level purity"). RockBase-family
  mineable + a single mined item, same pattern this repo already established
  twice this sitting: src/RimUtinni/UtinniPatches's RUT_GreatboleHeartwood
  (wall ParentName="RockBase", mineable=true, building.mineableThing + yield
  + dropChance, mineableScatterCommonality=0 — deliberate placement, not
  vanilla's own ore-vein scatterer) and src/RimMandrake/FloodedCanyon's
  RM_FossilSeams (same shape, cited there as cribbed from this precedent).

  Placement is OWED, not this file's scope: BLUEDESERT_MECHANICS_BUILD_1 §2's
  quarry injection templates are the sheet's own home for it ("Injectable
  ancient ice quarries" §8) — same restraint both precedents above already
  took. mineableScatterCommonality stays 0 until that GenStep (or a
  biome-scoped ore-vein patch) exists; shipping this def with real placement
  authored by no one would be a silent-guess violation of the same kind this
  repo's CLAUDE.md warns against elsewhere.

  The thaw roll (mechanics item §3: "every N blocks cut rolls nothing / a
  fallen-debris find / a release") is RimMandrake.BlueDesert.
  CompProperties_BlueIceThaw below (Source/RM_BlueIceThaw.cs), DEBRIS-ONLY
  as the item rules: the release table joins when HORRORS_RAIDING_FACTION_1
  lands. blocksPerRoll 3, debrisChance 0.35 and the debris table are this
  build's INVENTED tuning toward §5's "the fallen" (meteoritic metal, crashed
  wreckage); the freeze-dried orbital dead are §8's ablation-line incident
  family, not this roll, so the two never double-author "the fallen".

  ART: no separate wall-face texture was commissioned (art commission table,
  bluedesert_bedazzle_cast_2026-09-28.md §7: only the item icon, "mineable
  atlas derives from it"). Per the bible's own fallback ("FOUNDRY derives the
  atlas tile from the item art's material language if no separate wall
  texture is commissioned"), the wall reuses the commissioned item PNG
  directly (Graphic_Single, no linked-tile atlas) rather than a vanilla
  rock texture — a real, non-placeholder texture, just not yet a dedicated
  16-cell atlas. A bespoke wall atlas is owed art, not a def-correctness gap.
-->
<Defs>

  <ThingDef ParentName="RockBase">
    <defName>RM_BlueIceMineable</defName>
    <label>blue ice</label>
    <description>Ancient water ice compressed so long and so clean that it is finally, truly blue in the body -- the one thing in the Blue Desert that is not a trick of scattering. Cut into it and the wall face weeps cold light for a moment before it goes dark again.</description>
    <graphicData>
      <texPath>Things/Item/Resource/RM_BlueIce/RM_BlueIce</texPath>
      <graphicClass>Graphic_Single</graphicClass>
    </graphicData>
    <statBases>
      <MaxHitPoints>260</MaxHitPoints>
    </statBases>
    <mineable>true</mineable>
    <building>
      <isResourceRock>true</isResourceRock>
      <mineableThing>RM_BlueIce</mineableThing>
      <mineableYield>15</mineableYield>
      <mineableDropChance>1</mineableDropChance>
      <veinMineable>false</veinMineable>
      <mineableScatterCommonality>0</mineableScatterCommonality>
    </building>
    <comps>
      <li Class="RimMandrake.BlueDesert.CompProperties_BlueIceThaw">
        <blocksPerRoll>3</blocksPerRoll>
        <debrisChance>0.35</debrisChance>
        <debris>
          <li>
            <thing>Steel</thing>
            <count>15~40</count>
            <weight>4</weight>
          </li>
          <li>
            <thing>ChunkSlagSteel</thing>
            <count>1~1</count>
            <weight>3</weight>
          </li>
          <li>
            <thing>ComponentIndustrial</thing>
            <count>1~3</count>
            <weight>2</weight>
          </li>
          <li>
            <thing>Plasteel</thing>
            <count>5~15</count>
            <weight>1</weight>
          </li>
        </debris>
      </li>
    </comps>
  </ThingDef>

</Defs>
